---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/docs.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Git and package remotes — Querial">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-querial-sky">For DevOps</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-1">Git and package remotes</h1>
            <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground max-w-3xl">
                Git is an optional distribution and collaboration channel for
                <code class="text-foreground">querial.package/v1</code> trees. It is not
                the control plane, not the scheduler, and not required to design, publish,
                deploy, or run a pipeline.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground [&>h3]:text-foreground">
            <h2>What Git is for</h2>
            <p>
                A Git repository row in a project points at a host working directory
                (and optionally an HTTPS remote). Querial can clone, fetch, commit a
                normalized package tree, optionally push, compare two stored revisions,
                and activate a revision into the <strong class="text-foreground">current
                project as drafts</strong>. You still publish immutable pipeline versions
                in Workspace. Git never executes SQL. Agents never need a remote.
            </p>
            <p>
                Control-database authority stays with Querial: deployments, encrypted
                endpoints, users, agents, schedules, runs, and API keys never belong in
                Git. Promotion copies the same pipeline version (and, when present, the
                recorded Git commit and package checksum) to another environment kind —
                it does not re-enter secrets.
            </p>

            <h2>Two different “publishes”</h2>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto not-prose">
                <table class="w-full text-sm">
                    <thead><tr class="border-b border-border text-left"><th class="py-2 pr-4">Action</th><th class="py-2">What it freezes</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
                        <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-foreground">Pipeline Publish (Workspace)</td><td class="py-2">Immutable DAG in the control database. Required to deploy and run. Independent of Git.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-foreground">Publish to Git</td><td class="py-2">Export the package tree, secret-scan, commit (write modes), optional push. Records commit hash + tree SHA-256. Explicit operator/API action — not automatic on pipeline publish.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h2>Feature flag</h2>
            <p>
                All Git screens and APIs are gated by
                <code class="text-foreground">Querial:Extensions:GitIntegration</code>
                (environment form <code class="text-foreground">Querial__Extensions__GitIntegration</code>).
                Stock defaults are off. Local AppHost turns the flag on; it still does
                <em>not</em> invent a working directory. With the flag off, Git
                Repositories shows a disabled message, list APIs return empty, and
                mutating Git calls fail closed with a clear error. Draft → publish →
                deploy → run is unchanged.
            </p>

            <h2>Who configures it</h2>
            <p>
                Git Repositories is a Workspace screen under Design, shown when this
                role has CanOperate. Developers do not see it. The API is
                <code class="text-foreground">/api/git-repositories</code> under the same
                project-operator policy. Rows are <strong class="text-foreground">project-scoped</strong>:
                names are unique per project. There is no instance-wide “Git root.”
            </p>

            <h2>Repository modes</h2>
            <p>
                Each row has a mode. Disabled means the row exists but Sync/publish
                refuse work.
            </p>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto not-prose">
                <table class="w-full text-sm">
                    <thead><tr class="border-b border-border text-left"><th class="py-2 pr-4">Mode</th><th class="py-2 pr-4">Remote</th><th class="py-2 pr-4">Working tree</th><th class="py-2">Push</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
                        <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-foreground">disabled</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">—</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">—</td><td class="py-2">—</td></tr>
                        <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-foreground">local_rw</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">optional</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Local path required. Init if missing.</td><td class="py-2">Never</td></tr>
                        <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-foreground">external_rw</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">HTTPS URL required</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Local path or Base path</td><td class="py-2">Optional on publish-to-Git</td></tr>
                        <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-foreground">external_ro</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">HTTPS URL required</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Local path or Base path</td><td class="py-2">Never</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-foreground">central_client</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">HTTPS URL required</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Local path or Base path</td><td class="py-2">Never (fetch/activate only)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p>
                Fetch-only modes log that the remote will not be written. Treat any
                push ability on those modes as a misconfiguration.
            </p>
            <p>
                <code class="text-foreground">central_client</code> still uses the
                ordinary Sync path (clone/fetch + validate). A dedicated “import every
                central revision” endpoint exists on the API and currently returns
                <strong class="text-foreground">501</strong> — it is fail-closed, not a
                silent success. Activate a fetched tree the same way as
                <code class="text-foreground">external_ro</code>.
            </p>

            <h2>Working directory</h2>
            <p>
                Resolved per row:
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>If <strong class="text-foreground">Local path</strong> is set → that absolute directory (preferred).</li>
                <li>Else if <strong class="text-foreground">Base path</strong> is set → <code class="text-foreground">{"{BasePath}/{sanitized-name}"}</code>.</li>
                <li>Else → configuration error; no tree can be materialized.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                Name sanitization keeps letters, digits, <code class="text-foreground">.</code>,
                <code class="text-foreground">_</code>, <code class="text-foreground">-</code>.
                Other characters become hyphens. <code class="text-foreground">local_rw</code>
                requires Local path. Fetch modes require a remote URL. Branch defaults
                to <code class="text-foreground">main</code> when blank on sync.
            </p>
            <p>
                The Web/API process identity must be able to create and write that
                path. Querial does not mount a volume for you. Prefer a durable host
                folder (for local AppHost, a repo-relative <code class="text-foreground">.local/git/…</code>
                is a common choice — not the artifact temp directory).
            </p>

            <h2>Transport</h2>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>Embedded Git library (libgit2 bindings). No system <code class="text-foreground">git</code> install for v1 HTTPS and local modes.</li>
                <li>Any standard Git host over the Git protocol. Querial does not call host REST APIs and does not automate pull requests or merge requests.</li>
                <li>Auth is HTTPS username + password/token. Username is taken from the URL userinfo when present, otherwise <code class="text-foreground">git</code>. The token is AES-GCM encrypted at rest (same protector as other secrets) with AAD <code class="text-foreground">git_repo:{"{id}"}:remote_auth</code>. Never written into package files or logs (URLs with userinfo are masked).</li>
                <li>SSH agent / corporate SSH is out of scope for v1.</li>
                <li>Clone checks out the configured branch. Subsequent Sync on an existing working copy <strong class="text-foreground">fetches</strong> <code class="text-foreground">origin/{"{branch}"}</code> (prune, tags). It does not merge or reset the working tree by itself.</li>
                <li>Commit stages all tracked changes. If the tree is clean, the current HEAD SHA is returned (no empty commit). Push is only for <code class="text-foreground">external_rw</code> when requested.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Package layouts in the working tree</h2>
            <p>
                Detected from files on disk. Mixing layouts in one working directory is
                <strong class="text-foreground">ambiguous</strong> and rejected.
            </p>
            <h3>Package-root</h3>
            <pre class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>LocalPath/
  querial.package.yaml
  connections/catalog.yaml
  pipelines/
    &lt;pipeline.code&gt;/
      pipeline.yaml
      …</code></pre>
            <p>
                The working directory <em>is</em> the package. Existing package-root
                remotes keep this layout: publish-to-Git replaces the tree at the root
                (never deletes <code class="text-foreground">.git</code>).
            </p>
            <h3>Workspace</h3>
            <pre class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>LocalPath/
  packages/
    aw-scenario-a/
      querial.package.yaml
      …
    aw-scenario-b/
      querial.package.yaml
      …</code></pre>
            <p>
                One Git repo, many packages. Publish writes only
                <code class="text-foreground">packages/&lt;package.code&gt;/</code> and
                leaves siblings alone. Sync validates every
                <code class="text-foreground">packages/*/querial.package.yaml</code>.
                Activate resolves by stored package code (or the sole package if there
                is exactly one). Folder names cannot contain
                <code class="text-foreground">..</code>, slashes, or backslashes.
            </p>
            <h3>Empty</h3>
            <p>
                No manifests yet. Sync succeeds with a warning. The first publish-to-Git
                creates <code class="text-foreground">packages/&lt;package.code&gt;/</code>
                (workspace), not a root manifest — unless you already placed a
                package-root tree.
            </p>
            <p>
                Zip import/export remains <strong class="text-foreground">one package
                root per archive</strong>. A zip is a snapshot of a tree; Git is the
                living copy of that tree. Author against the directory, not the zip
                bytes. Tree shape:
                <a href="/docs/developer-packages" class="text-link hover:underline">Packages</a>.
            </p>

            <h2>Sync</h2>
            <p>
                Workspace: Sync now on repository details. API:
                <code class="text-foreground">POST /api/git-repositories/{"{id}"}/sync</code>.
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li><code class="text-foreground">local_rw</code> — create the directory, init if needed, validate the package layout, report HEAD.</li>
                <li>Fetch modes — clone if the path is not a repo; otherwise fetch the branch. Then validate the layout. Fetch-only messages state the remote was not modified. Write-mode Sync still does not push.</li>
                <li>History rows record fetch vs checkout, branch, status, actor, timestamps.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Invalid trees fail Sync (ambiguous mix, schema errors). Empty trees are
                allowed until the first publish-to-Git.
            </p>

            <h2>Publish to Git</h2>
            <p>
                API: <code class="text-foreground">POST /api/git-repositories/publish</code>
                with a pipeline id, version number, optional repository id, package
                code/name, commit message, and <code class="text-foreground">pushToRemote</code>
                (default true; ignored unless <code class="text-foreground">external_rw</code>).
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>Export the pipeline(s) as a package zip and unpack to a temp workspace.</li>
                <li>Validate the tree (same rules as Package CLI / zip import).</li>
                <li>Secret-scan the export. Known secret field names and connection-string heuristics fail closed.</li>
                <li>Compute tree SHA-256.</li>
                <li>Ensure the working repo (clone or init; fetch on existing <code class="text-foreground">external_rw</code>).</li>
                <li>Copy into the publish destination (wipe destination except <code class="text-foreground">.git</code>).</li>
                <li>Scan again on the destination. Commit. Optional push.</li>
                <li>Insert a package-version row: commit hash, checksum, branch/tag, previous active id. The new row is <em>not</em> active until you activate.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                Without a repository id, the API can still record a version from supplied
                package content after a secret scan — useful for tests; it is not a Git
                commit.
            </p>

            <h2>Activate</h2>
            <p>
                Takes a stored package version and imports the matching on-disk tree
                into the current project as <strong class="text-foreground">drafts</strong>.
                Workspace UI: repository details, with the same connection and
                schema-source resolution wizard as zip import (use existing / create /
                remap). API:
                <code class="text-foreground">POST /api/git-repositories/versions/{"{id}"}/activate</code>.
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>Working directory must exist and contain <code class="text-foreground">querial.package.yaml</code> at the resolved package path.</li>
                <li>On-disk tree SHA-256 must equal the stored checksum. If it does not, Sync or check out the recorded commit first — activate will not silently import a dirty or drifted tree.</li>
                <li>Workspace remotes with several packages need the stored package code.</li>
                <li>Unresolved connections or schema sources return a session (Web JSON / API 422) instead of writing drafts.</li>
                <li>On success, the previous active package version is deactivated. Pipeline Publish in Workspace remains a separate gate.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Rollback</h2>
            <p>
                Restores the previous immutable <em>package-version</em> pointer
                (<code class="text-foreground">previous_version_id</code>), not a Git
                <code class="text-foreground">reset --hard</code>. API:
                <code class="text-foreground">POST /api/git-repositories/pipelines/{"{id}"}/rollback</code>.
                Fails if there is no active version or no previous record. Audit stores
                from/to version numbers and checksum.
            </p>

            <h2>Diff</h2>
            <p>
                <code class="text-foreground">GET /api/git-repositories/diff?versionA=&amp;versionB=</code>.
                Modes, in order:
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li><code class="text-foreground">checksum</code> — identical tree hashes → no entries.</li>
                <li><code class="text-foreground">git-tree</code> — both versions have commit SHAs in the same repository and those commits exist in the working clone. File-level added/removed/modified/renamed plus a truncated unified patch (cap 65,536 characters).</li>
                <li><code class="text-foreground">metadata</code> — fallback JSON compare of stored package content when the working tree or hashes are missing.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                If diffs fall back to metadata, re-sync or publish through Git so both
                commits exist locally.
            </p>

            <h2>Secrets</h2>
            <p>
                Packages must not contain passwords, tokens, API keys, ciphertext,
                connection strings, or similarly named fields. The scanner runs on
                export, again immediately before commit, and on zip import. SQL and
                markdown skip the connection-string heuristic (so a comment that
                mentions <code class="text-foreground">Password</code> is less likely
                to false-positive) but YAML keys named like secrets still fail.
                Credentials for remotes live only in the encrypted credential store.
            </p>

            <h2>Audit and status</h2>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>Repository status: unknown, ok, syncing, error, disconnected.</li>
                <li>Sync history on the details page and <code class="text-foreground">GET …/sync-history</code>.</li>
                <li>Publication audit: commit, activate, rollback — <code class="text-foreground">GET …/{"{id}"}/audit</code>.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>API surface (session, current project)</h2>
            <pre class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>{`GET    /api/git-repositories
POST   /api/git-repositories
PUT    /api/git-repositories/{id}
DELETE /api/git-repositories/{id}
GET    /api/git-repositories/{id}/status
POST   /api/git-repositories/{id}/sync
GET    /api/git-repositories/{id}/sync-history
GET    /api/git-repositories/{id}/imports
GET    /api/git-repositories/{id}/audit
POST   /api/git-repositories/publish
POST   /api/git-repositories/versions/{versionId}/activate
GET    /api/git-repositories/diff?versionA=&versionB=
GET    /api/git-repositories/pipelines/{pipelineId}/versions
POST   /api/git-repositories/pipelines/{pipelineId}/rollback
POST   /api/git-repositories/{id}/central-fetch   → 501 until implemented`}</code></pre>
            <p>
                Cookie callers still need CSRF on mutating verbs. API keys are not
                Platform admin and are not this Git surface.
            </p>

            <h2>What Git never does</h2>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>Replace pipeline Publish, deployments, or runs.</li>
                <li>Carry connection endpoints, schema-source fetch strings, or API keys.</li>
                <li>Assign agents or choose pools.</li>
                <li>Require a remote for air-gapped or single-instance installs.</li>
                <li>Use SSH in v1, or host-specific pull-request APIs.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
